// C2 / ADR 0017 D11 + ADR 0016, register D37: a generic fn's type parameter behind a
// REFERENCE (`fn takes<T>(x: &T)`) and behind a `Vec<T>`.
//
// `unify_one` had arms for type-param, array, nullable, secret and generic-instance
// params, and none for a REF or a VEC — so `T` was never bound at all. The unbound `-1`
// then reached the mangler, where `scg` used to die outright: `index out of bounds:
// idx=-101`, exit 127, zero bytes emitted, on programs the oracle compiles cleanly.
// (That abort is register D58 and is separately guarded now; this file pins the
// inference itself.)
//
// ⚠ THE ARMS MIRROR THE ORACLE'S PREDICATE, NOT ITS EFFECT, and that distinction is the
// whole difficulty. `crates/sentinel-types`'s ref arm fires ONLY on (Ref, Ref) and only
// when the two MUTABILITIES match; anything else falls to its trailing `Mismatch`.
// Binding unconditionally would look identical on every program here and would make
// `scg` bind `T` for four shapes the oracle REJECTS — `&mut T` given `&a`, `&T` given
// `&mut a`, `&T` given a non-ref, `Vec<T>` given `[i64]`. All four were constructed and
// all four are oracle-rejected, which means **the codegen differential could never catch
// the over-acceptance**: it only runs oracle-accepted programs. The predicate has to be
// copied because nothing downstream would notice if it were not.
//
// ⚠ THE `Vec<T>` ARM DOES NOT ROUTE THROUGH `array_elem_of`, even though that helper is
// right there and answers the element for both kinds. It is deliberately uniform over
// `[T]` and `Vec<T>` so `len`/`v[i]` work on either — correct for INDEXING and wrong for
// UNIFICATION, where the oracle refuses to mix them in both directions (constructed).
// The neighbouring array arm does route through it and therefore over-binds `[T]` from a
// `Vec<i64>` argument; pre-existing, unobservable for the same reason, and recorded
// rather than "made consistent".
//
// 1 + 1 + 40 = 42.
fn by_ref<T>(x: &T) -> i64 {
    1
}

fn by_vec<T>(v: Vec<T>) -> i64 {
    1
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    let a: i64 = 7;
    let mut v: Vec<i64> = vec_new();
    push(&mut v, 1);
    by_ref(&a) + by_vec(v) + 40
}
